Recurrent Urinary Tract Infections
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
More than 3 UTIs within 12 months, Recurrent dysuria/frequency, Positive urine cultures, Resolution between episodes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-038
Reason for Visit: Agonizing dysuria, profound urinary frequency, and macroscopic haematuria causing severe distress and complete sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported agonizing, razor-like pain upon micturition, passing drops of blood every ten minutes, and overwhelming urgency preventing any rest. Urgent after-hours assessment was explicitly required to confirm an acute lower urinary tract infection, exclude upper tract involvement, and initiate immediate targeted antibiotic and analgesic therapy to provide rapid symptomatic relief before routine daytime services open.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am passing drops of blood every ten minutes, it feels like razor blades, and I am exhausted from sitting on the toilet all night.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an acute, severe flare of recurrent dysuria, characterized by an agonizing burning sensation during micturition. They experience profound urinary frequency and a sudden, compelling urgency. They note suprapubic pain and a constant sensation of incomplete bladder emptying. Tonight, the urine is distinctly cloudy, foul-smelling, and contains visible macroscopic haematuria. They explicitly state they have had four culture-proven symptomatic urinary tract infections within the last twelve months, with complete resolution of symptoms between infective episodes. They note these infections frequently exacerbate following sexual intercourse. They strictly deny any high fevers, rigors, or severe back pain.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired by extreme exhaustion and the absolute necessity to remain on or near the toilet.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute sleep deprivation and distress related to the visible blood in the urine.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.2C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, highly anxious regarding the bleeding, shifting uncomfortably and requesting to use the bathroom during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Mild to moderate suprapubic tenderness upon deep palpation. Back: Complete absence of costovertebral angle tenderness bilaterally. Genitourinary: Point-of-care midstream urinalysis is strongly positive for nitrites, leukocyte esterase, and marked macroscopic haematuria. Expected investigation results will feature positive midstream urine cultures identifying the specific causative uropathogen, alongside a normal future renal tract ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; multiple water glasses present as the patient has been attempting to flush their system.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prophylactic antibiotics. Over-the-counter urinary alkalizers provided minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Recurrent Urinary Tract Infections - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis Flare)
Differentials:

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit absence of high systemic fevers, rigors, and negative costovertebral angle tenderness on examination.

Interstitial Cystitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the point-of-care urinalysis is floridly positive for bacterial infection markers (nitrites/leukocytes), whereas interstitial cystitis features sterile urine.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strict absence of colicky, agonizing unilateral flank pain radiating to the groin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a severe, acute exacerbation of recurrent uncomplicated bacterial cystitis. The classical presentation of agonizing dysuria, relentless frequency, and positive nitrite/leukocyte dipstick confirms the diagnosis. The presence of macroscopic haematuria, while alarming to the patient, is a common feature of severe mucosal inflammation. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was mandated to definitively rule out ascending upper tract infection (pyelonephritis) which would require parenteral therapy, safely interpret the urinalysis, and immediately initiate targeted oral antibiotics. Deferring this care would subject the patient to an unacceptable duration of agonizing pain and risk ascending septic complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced oral Nitrofurantoin 100mg twice daily for 5 days. Provided a prescription for dedicated urinary alkalizers and advised regular paracetamol for analgesia. Collected a formal midstream urine sample to be sent for comprehensive microscopy, culture, and sensitivities in the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To dramatically reduce the incidence of future recurrent infections, the patient is instructed to implement a strict post-coital voiding routine combined with drinking a large glass of water immediately after intimacy. Experiencing a sustained, measurable drop in the frequency of these agonizing flares will act as powerful positive reinforcement, permanently cementing this critical hygienic habit into their sexual routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to review formal urine culture sensitivities and discuss long-term prophylactic strategies or urological imaging.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review or Emergency Department presentation is required if the patient develops high fevers, uncontrollable shivering, severe back pain, or intractable vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The antibiotics cover the most common bacteria, but the treatment may need to be adjusted based on the final laboratory culture results in two days.
Clinical Course: The agonizing burning and frequency should significantly improve within 24 to 48 hours of starting the antibiotics. The visible blood will fade as the inflammation settles.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a high fever, start shaking with chills, or feel intense pain in your mid-back, you must seek urgent medical care as the infection may have spread up to your kidneys.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria have caused a severe inflammation of your bladder lining, causing it to bleed slightly and spasm constantly, resulting in the agonizing pain and urgency. Because this is happening recurrently, we must treat it aggressively. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria in the urine, while the alkalizers will reduce the acidity that causes the burning sensation.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire 5-day course of antibiotics even if you feel completely better tomorrow. Drink at least two liters of water daily. Urinate immediately after sexual intercourse moving forward.

